# @LOOM_PROJECT_NAME@

This application was generated by [loom](https://github.com/Arcadyi/loom) with the
[loom](https://github.com/Arcadyi/loom) styling library pre-wired.

```sh
loom doctor
loom dev
```

Edit `qml/Main.qml` while the application is running. QML, JavaScript, and asset
changes reload in the existing process. C++ and build-system changes trigger an
incremental rebuild and restart.

## Styling with loom

`qml/Main.qml` uses both of loom's layers:

```qml
import Loom

// Typed design tokens.
Label { color: Loom.color.foreground }

// Utility strings, with state and responsive variants.
Rectangle { Lo.style: "bg-surface rounded-lg hover:bg-surface-alt transition-colors" }
```

`Loom.theme = "dark"` switches every semantic token at runtime; this project
already persists the theme across hot reloads via `loomSaveState`.

## Finding loom

`find_package(loom CONFIG REQUIRED)` searches CMake's usual prefixes. If loom is
installed somewhere else — including straight out of its build tree — put that
path on `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` before running any loom command:

```sh
export CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/loom/build   # or an install prefix
loom build
```

CMake reads that environment variable natively, and loom's child processes
inherit it, so `build`, `test`, `lint` and `dev` all pick it up.
